.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-c1qsc6_macos-signed-distribution/TASK-260715-1gzhnk_generate-macos-provenance-checksums-and-compliance-bundle/README.md:7:In scope: artifact and nested-code hashes, source and dependency materials, build and signing invocations, generator and Xcode versions, release versions, public signing identity and profile metadata, entitlement reports, notarization request and ticket, Gatekeeper output, relay staging attestations, SBOM and notice references, install tests, approvals, retention, attestation, and evidence index. Out of scope: private keys, passwords, issuer secrets, unredacted notarization logs, user data, iOS evidence, and replacing independent verification.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-3pv7qc_relay-portable-build-assets/TASK-260715-1ue4oy_generate-and-embed-relay-asset-manifest/README.md:10:1. A deterministic generator emits one schema-validated entry for each of the four assets and no unsupported or duplicate platform tuple. 2. Every recorded size and SHA-256 is computed from the exact bundled bytes and every build identity matches the executable response or handshake contract. 3. Bundle validation fails on missing, extra, renamed, duplicate, zero-length, hash-mismatched, protocol-mismatched, or unparseable assets before runtime selection. 4. The Apple client consumes a generated or validated typed lookup without caller-supplied file paths, mutable manifest data, or network access. 5. Tests cover all valid tuples, common uname normalization handoff values, schema version mismatch, tampering, deterministic regeneration, and generated-resource inclusion in both platform products.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-14lk3y_preserve-legacy-swiftpm-app-and-release-path/README.md:4:Adapt repository build entry points only as needed so the generated workspace can coexist with the current ReluxProxy SwiftPM executable, tests, menu-bar app packaging, DMG creation, defaults, and release artifact contract.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-14lk3y_preserve-legacy-swiftpm-app-and-release-path/README.md:7:In scope: coexistence of Package.swift and generated workspace; existing swift build and swift test; Makefile commands; app and DMG scripts; universal build expectation; Info.plist and default values; stable ReluxProxy.dmg naming; explicit deprecation boundary; regression checks. Out of scope: refactoring legacy source, sandboxing system SSH, changing user-visible defaults, rewriting release signing, adding iOS release automation, or retiring the current product.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-anxje6_continuous-integration-quality-gates/TASK-260715-2759wy_validate-semantic-versions-tags-and-release-notes/README.md:7:In scope: semantic tag format, monotonic version rules, iOS build numbers, macOS bundle versions, relay protocol and build identity compatibility, generated workspace inputs, artifact naming, stable versus versioned assets, release-note sections, breaking or migration disclosures, and dry-run validation. Out of scope: choosing product launch timing, allocating App Store Connect numbers, publishing releases, and writing marketing copy beyond required technical sections.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-anxje6_continuous-integration-quality-gates/TASK-260715-3mk4hs_validate-generated-workspace-and-legacy-swiftpm-product/README.md:1:# Validate generated workspace determinism and the preserved SwiftPM product
.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-anxje6_continuous-integration-quality-gates/TASK-260715-3mk4hs_validate-generated-workspace-and-legacy-swiftpm-product/README.md:10:1. A clean runner installs or resolves the pinned generator, regenerates the workspace, and fails on any unexplained tracked diff or missing approved target, scheme, configuration, or deployment target. 2. Credential-free builds cover every approved generated scheme that can build without production identities and clearly mark signing-only checks as not executed. 3. The existing SwiftPM tests and executable build pass and the legacy application or DMG packaging contract remains reproducible at its declared boundary. 4. Logs record source revision, generator, Xcode, Swift, SDK, deployment targets, schemes, dependency locks, and commands without secrets. 5. Controlled target removal, generator drift, identifier drift, legacy regression, and dirty-generation fixtures fail the gate.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-3pv7qc_relay-portable-build-assets/TASK-260715-vtot05_add-relay-license-provenance-and-supply-chain-metadata/README.md:10:1. Every source and build dependency affecting relay bytes maps to a pinned revision or content hash, license identifier, notice obligation, and provenance entry. 2. Required relay and transitive notices are generated into the product notice input and an automated test fails when a dependency lacks approved metadata. 3. The artifact manifest references the matching source and build provenance without embedding credentials, workstation paths, private repository tokens, or mutable latest URLs. 4. A supply-chain boundary table assigns M2 source pinning, build reproducibility, asset hashes, and notices while assigning app signing, notarization, release attestation, and distribution approval to concrete M5 scope. 5. A clean audit command verifies locks, hashes, notice coverage, inventory consistency, and zero application-runtime code download.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/TASK-260728-7ii1xz_decide-macos-sysex-credential-transport/README.md:4:Decide how one configured SSH profile's private key and optional passphrase reach the macOS packet-tunnel provider, given that the provider is a system extension running as root outside a user context. TASK-260715-ypo7yo established from Apple documentation and DTS guidance that the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code running in a user context and that keychain access groups share items between programs running as the SAME user. The macOS host runs as the logged-in user and the provider runs as root, so the .spec/security-privacy.md design (secrets in the Data Protection Keychain shared through one access group with the packet tunnel extension) cannot work on macOS as written. iOS is unaffected because its appex runs as the same user as its host. This task selects the macOS transport, states its security properties honestly, and defines the entitlement amendment it implies for the identifier matrix.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/TASK-260728-7ii1xz_decide-macos-sysex-credential-transport/README.md:10:1. The artifact states the exact open question verbatim and cites the two Apple-sourced facts that create it: a macOS NE system extension runs as root outside a user context, and the Data Protection Keychain plus keychain access groups only share items between programs running as the same user. 2. At least three candidate transports are compared on unattended/on-demand start with the containing app not running, secret lifetime and zeroization, blast radius if the provider is compromised, entitlement and portal cost, and testability; each candidate states what it CANNOT guarantee. 3. One transport is selected with rationale, or the task records a concrete blocker with options, tradeoffs, and a recommendation rather than a guessed design. 4. The selection produces an explicit amendment verdict for keychain-access-groups on works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel in the TASK-260715-ypo7yo matrix: either grant with the exact literal, or keep prohibited with the reason; the verdict names TASK-260715-ypo7yo as the applying owner. 5. Where documentation alone cannot settle reachability, a privacy-safe physical check on this Apple-silicon Mac records the observed result with commands and exit codes and no secret value; where no check was possible the limitation is named, never assumed away. 6. The affected downstream tasks TASK-260715-379cpk, TASK-260715-1o9wjz, TASK-260715-29ws8l, TASK-260715-2hhh7x, and TASK-260715-3f4lxy are listed with the specific assumption each must revise.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2m2tl1_m0-viability-manual-gates/TASK-260728-q5kjta_conduct-c1-apple-permission-ceremony/README.md:7:In scope under the resumed-evidence amendment, within this one ceremony node: establishing effective prompt-free private-key access for the Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities to the signing tools that run unattended; confirming an authenticated Xcode or Apple Developer portal context for the Relux Works team and the exact macOS-only matrix authority, while honestly recording whether a separate two-factor timestamp exists and any later reauthentication risk; authorizing creation and download of exactly the macOS entries in the approved identifier and entitlement matrix (revision 2026-07-28.r12) and nothing beyond them, namely the four macOS App IDs works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac and works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel, the Network Extensions capability on those four and no other capability, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered as a device; explicitly NOT creating any App Group record, NOT enabling the App Groups capability on any App ID, and NOT performing any Keychain access group mutation, because matrix rule G4 prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, rule K2 prohibits keychain-access-groups on every macOS target, and keychain sharing has no portal record at all; storing a NAMED notarytool profile in the login Keychain; choosing and stating the owner disposition for the source App Store Connect API private-key file; generating the Sparkle ed25519 keypair with the pinned vendor tool and placing the private key into approved custody; recording only non-secret grant facts and honest provenance. Harmless command-name-only shell history is permitted, but secret values, secret-bearing arguments, identifiers, account credentials, key bytes, and secret paths are prohibited everywhere. Out of scope: approving any system VPN or system extension dialog (that is Approval A1 and later Hold H2, and no signed artifact exists yet); provisioning the deferred iOS identifiers; creating any Developer ID or App Store distribution profile; performing the portal mutations themselves (TASK-260715-3jloqy); validating profiles, verifying notary authentication, or recording public-key evidence (owned by the downstream evidence tasks); any product code; any owner product decision.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2m2tl1_m0-viability-manual-gates/TASK-260728-q5kjta_conduct-c1-apple-permission-ceremony/README.md:10:1. Ceremony C1 remains exactly one up-front human-input board node. Evidence may be collected across resumed owner interactions with producer or reviewer cycles between them, provided the record gives every known interaction date or time, states which boundaries were not captured, identifies the intervening cycles, and makes no uninterrupted-session claim. The human is never asked to remain present through a producer or reviewer cycle. 2. Effective prompt-free private-key access is demonstrated for the Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities to the named unattended signing tools. The record names the identities by common name and the tools, never key material. Contemporaneous proof of the historical Always Allow click is not required; any provenance limitation is stated honestly. 3. An authenticated Xcode or Apple Developer portal context for the Relux Works team exists and the operator has confirmed authority to create the macOS entries in the approved matrix. A cached authenticated session is acceptable functional evidence without a separately timestamped two-factor event; the record states that limitation and any residual fresh-authentication or two-factor interaction the portal may force later. 4. Creation and download of exactly the approved macOS entries is authorized and nothing beyond them: the four macOS App IDs works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac and works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel; the Network Extensions capability on those four; and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Mac registered. The record states explicitly that NO App Group record was created, that the App Groups capability was enabled on no App ID, that no Keychain access group portal mutation was performed or required, and that no Developer ID or App Store distribution profile was created. All FOUR iOS App IDs (works.relux.tunnel.ios, works.relux.tunnel.ios.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.ios, works.relux.tunnel.probe.ios.tunnel) are explicitly NOT authorized because iOS is deferred under ADR-024, and works.relux.proxy is not touched. 5. A NAMED notarytool credential profile is stored in the login Keychain and the owner states the disposition for the source App Store Connect API private-key file; only the profile name and the chosen disposition are recorded, never the file path, key ID, issuer ID, passphrase, or key bytes. 6. The Sparkle ed25519 keypair is generated with the pinned vendor tool and the private key is placed in approved custody; only the custody location name is recorded here. 7. Credential values and secret-bearing arguments are never echoed or recorded. A post-ceremony scan of the repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history finds no secret value, secret path, key ID, issuer ID, account credential, or key bytes. Harmless command-name-only history is permitted and must be reported honestly rather than suppressed from the evidence. 8. A TASK-ID-scoped outcome records which grants succeeded, which were declined, honest interaction provenance, and any residual interaction the portal may still force later; a declined grant is recorded as a named blocker on the specific downstream task rather than as an assumed grant.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-2dtdql_release-operations-privacy-and-review/TASK-260715-1kpnkl_provision-isolated-app-review-ssh-test-environment/README.md:10:1. The fixture is isolated from production, uses a dedicated minimum-privilege account and keys, exposes only required SSH access, and records approved host-key algorithms and SHA-256 fingerprints. 2. Review can complete documented full and degraded tests with bounded resources, while no credential grants shell or infrastructure privileges beyond the required relay and test behavior. 3. Credentials and fingerprints are delivered only through approved secure review mechanisms, never committed, logged, placed in provider configuration, or attached to public board resources. 4. Health, expiration, rotation, compromise, abuse, rate-limit, relay install, disk full, noexec or read-only simulation where needed, and teardown procedures have named owners and alerts. 5. A safe rehearsal from a clean external device follows the same instructions, succeeds without production state, and verifies that logs and monitoring contain no packet payload, DNS name, destination, passphrase, or private key.
