423:## 7. Credential lifetime, passphrase, cancellation, and clearing
424-
425-1. The host keeps seed-message secret bytes only through message encode/send/
426-   response or cancellation, then overwrites owned mutable buffers and releases
427:   all references. A staging Keychain item may survive interruption only under
428-   the explicit M4 staging lifecycle; successful provider storage deletes it.
429-2. The provider copies message bytes into the minimum mutable buffers needed to
430-   validate and store, then overwrites them and releases references after the
431:   Keychain result. It never caches seed requests.
432:3. The credential resolver retrieves item data only after host trust is
433:   approved. It decodes credential generation, format, key, and optional
434:   passphrase inside one lexical/asynchronous credential scope.
435:4. If the accepted key format is encrypted, decryption and passphrase handling
436:   occur inside that scope. A passphrase is never returned as a standalone
437-   public value. The selected SSH adapter receives public-key material and an
438:   external signing closure, not private-key or passphrase bytes.
439-5. Cancellation before a synchronous Security operation prevents it from
440-   starting. Once a Security call has begun it is not claimed cancellable; a
441-   cancellation observed on return discards the result, clears owned buffers,
442-   and prevents authentication. Cancellation during authentication retires the
443-   signer and clears/relinquishes its state before bootstrap cleanup completes.
--
448-   best-effort clearing, never perfect zeroization.
